#      Whetherever John knows whether Jane went.

=3D ""Either (1a) Jane went and John knows Jane went OR (1b) Jane=20
didn't go and John knows Jane didn't go OR (2a) Jane went and=20
John does not know Jane went OR (2b) Jane didn't go and John doesn't know J=
ane didn't go"

#What am I missing?

The meaning I was trying to get, is a qkau version of=20
"la djan djuno le du'u xu la djein klama". Let's change it to
{jinvi} to make things less confusing:

"la djan jinvi le du'u xu pau la djein klama"

This asks whether John believes Jane did go, or whether John
believes Jane didn't go. It ought to be possible to form a=20
main clause whetherever from this, but it isn't.

A similar example would be

  "However many people John reckons that I invited, he's still
got no right to issue invitations of his own"
=3D "Whatever the value of n such that John reckons that I
invited n people, ..."

